CHECK bb07.1 TRUE fixed basis: every designed dataset entered each column once, linearly (dirs 2), nrow(dirs) = ncol(x)+1
CHECK bb07.2 TRUE the intercept (term 1) is in every row of prune.terms (designed + random bases)
CHECK bb07.3 TRUE row 1 of prune.terms is the intercept alone
CHECK bb07.4 TRUE row k of prune.terms lists exactly k terms (row k is the subset of size k)
CHECK bb07.5 FALSE HYPOTHESIS rows of prune.terms are nested (size k subset inside size k+1 subset) in all designed datasets
CHECK bb07.6 FALSE HYPOTHESIS prune.terms equals my sequential backward elimination path in all designed datasets
CHECK bb07.7 FALSE HYPOTHESIS rss.per.subset equals the RSS of my backward path (rel 1e-10), all datasets
CHECK bb07.8 TRUE rss.per.subset[k] equals lm.fit RSS of earth's own prune.terms row k (rel 1e-10), all datasets
CHECK bb07.9 TRUE gcv.per.subset[k] equals get.gcv(rss, k, penalty, n) (rel 1e-10), all datasets
CHECK bb07.10 TRUE selected.terms equals the prune.terms row at which.min(gcv.per.subset), all datasets
CHECK bb07.11 TRUE weak-signal 12-column dataset D3 selects a size well inside the path
CHECK bb07.12 TRUE CORRECTED the prefix rule reproduces prune.terms in all designed and random bases
CHECK bb07.13 TRUE earth's rss.per.subset at each size <= RSS of my backward path at that size
CHECK bb07.13a FALSE ALTERNATIVE prefix rule with a swap (not a shift) reproduces prune.terms in all random bases
CHECK bb07.13b FALSE ALTERNATIVE 'forward-order prefix or backward-path subset, lower RSS' reproduces prune.terms in all random bases
CHECK bb07.14 TRUE pmethod='none' selects all K terms
CHECK bb07.15 TRUE pmethod='none' returns prune.terms, rss.per.subset, gcv.per.subset identical to pmethod='backward'
CHECK bb07.16 TRUE nprune=k does not shorten prune.terms, rss.per.subset or gcv.per.subset (all K sizes kept)
CHECK bb07.17 TRUE nprune=k selects the subset at which.min(gcv.per.subset[1:k]) of the fit without nprune
CHECK bb07.18 TRUE nprune=k: prune.terms rows and rss.per.subset for sizes <= k are identical to the fit without nprune
CHECK bb07.19 TRUE penalty=-1 with nprune=k selects exactly k terms
CHECK bb07.20 TRUE penalty=-1: gcv.per.subset equals rss.per.subset / n
CHECK bb07.21 TRUE penalty=-1 without nprune selects all K terms
CHECK bb07.22 TRUE pmethod='exhaustive': each prune.terms row is the brute-force lowest-RSS subset of its size
CHECK bb07.23 TRUE pmethod='exhaustive' differs from pmethod='backward' at some size, with lower RSS there
CHECK bb07.24 TRUE CORRECTED the prefix rule also reproduces prune.terms of ordinary earth fits (full hinge basis from dirs/cuts)
